# Every route from ordinary callable dispatch into an INTERPRETED closure, in
# one program -- the positive control for the PyClosureInvokeHook seam, which
# is what lets a program that mints no such closure drop the whole tree-walking
# evaluator (52% of the ESP NilPy image, measured 2026-09-20).
#
# THE SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT IS NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE. Most NilPy lambdas
# are LIFTED to compiled code and never touch the interpreter -- a fixture full
# of `key=lambda w: len(w)` passes identically with the hook removed, which I
# measured before writing this one. The lifter refuses exactly one thing: a
# capture of a managed STRING that is a LOCAL or PARAM of the enclosing
# function (bug-nilpy-lifted-lambda-cannot-capture-a-managed-string -- a string
# handle cannot ride a bound slot). That refusal falls back to
# pyclosure_src_new, which builds a pyeval closure from the body's SOURCE text.
# So every lambda below closes over a string local, and `make_*` exists solely
# to make that local a local. A module-level string is NOT enough: it resolves
# as a global and the lambda lifts.
#
# WHY IT CAN FAIL. With the hook uninstalled, PyCallClosureBody returns None
# rather than running the body, so each row still RUNS and prints a wrong
# answer -- sorting by None, a min() over Nones. Verified by removing the
# install and re-running: this file's output changes. A fixture whose lambdas
# lift cannot fail that way, which is exactly why the first version of this
# file was useless.
#
# Each row enters by a DIFFERENT door, because the seam has one call site per
# door and a fixture that exercises one certifies the others:
#   sorted(key=)              -> PyCallKey1
#   min/max, key in a VAR     -> PyCallKey1 via PyIterCallHook (pylib cannot
#                                see PyCallKey1 directly; keep that install
#                                unconditional -- a lazy one was
#                                bug-nilpy-min-max-with-a-key-held-in-a-variable)
#   map()/filter()            -> pymap_call / pyfilter_call
#   f(x) through a variable   -> pyvar_callv1

def make_keyer():
    # A string LOCAL. This is what stops the lambda being lifted.
    sep = "|"
    return lambda w: sep + str(len(w)) + sep + w


def make_upper():
    pad = ""
    return lambda w: pad + w.upper()


def make_longer_than(n):
    mark = ""
    return lambda w: len(mark + w) > n


def make_doubler():
    unit = ""
    return lambda v: unit + v + v


words = ["pear", "fig", "banana", "kiwi", "apple"]

# 1. sorted with an interpreted closure as key, both directions
keyer = make_keyer()
print(sorted(words, key=keyer))
print(sorted(words, key=keyer, reverse=True))

# 2. min/max reaching the same closure from pylib, key held in a variable
print(min(words, key=keyer), max(words, key=keyer))

# 3. map and filter over an interpreted closure
print(sorted(map(make_upper(), words)))
print(sorted(filter(make_longer_than(3), words)))

# 4. called directly through a variable
twice = make_doubler()
print(twice("ab"))

# 5. a second closure minted after the first has been called, so the row is not
#    just the first-ever closure working
print(sorted(["dd", "c", "bbb"], key=make_keyer()))
